  "title": "A client-chosen consumer name lands in requestedBy, which the factory trusts",
  "file": "src/integrations/mcp.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An MCP worker submits an ordinary application change using the consumer name dependabot[bot]. The factory applies dependency-bot routing and adds dependency-update review instructions solely because of that caller-chosen label.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun \"$TMPDIR/limitless-479-adjudication-hdecgl6u/probe.ts\" F1",
    "output": "{\"source\":\"mcp\",\"requestedBy\":\"dependabot[bot]\",\"taskClass\":null,\"freeFirstRouting\":true,\"dependencyUpdate_expression_at_engine_1408\":true,\"ownRunsCount\":1}"
   },
   "evidence": "The temporary probe calls the actual MCP server through its in-memory transport, persists the create request using the real Store, exercises the actual RunContext routing getter, and reads ownRuns. At HEAD, mcp.ts:299 forwards `requestedBy: consumer`. Store.insertRun persists `req.requestedBy ?? null` without namespacing. context.ts:276 checks `this.run.requestedBy === \"dependabot[bot]\" && this.deps.cfg.dependabotRouting === \"free_first\"`; free_first is the default configuration. engine.ts:1407-1408 sets `dependencyUpdate: ctx.run.taskClass === \"dependency_update\" || ctx.run.requestedBy === \"dependabot[bot]\"`. The review flag was established by source inspection and evaluating that expression, rather than running a complete pipeline. At base, MCP creation passed `{ ...input, source: \"mcp\" }` and its strict schema exposed neither consumer nor requestedBy. This establishes incorrect routing and review metadata, but does not demonstrate bypassing a gate or confinement boundary."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "An MCP agent that calls itself \"dependabot[bot]\" when creating a run gets that name stored as the run's requester. The factory then treats the run as a Dependabot update: it routes it free-first when that setting is on and adds dependency-update instructions to its review. Nothing is bypassed, because the loopback HTTP API already lets callers set that field directly.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun $TMPDIR/f2/f1.ts  (connects test/mcp-support.ts connect() to a stub backend that records the create input, then calls limitless_create_run with consumer \"dependabot[bot]\")",
    "output": "captured create input: {\"repo\":\"a/b\",\"prompt\":\"x\",\"profile\":\"auto\",\"requestedBy\":\"dependabot[bot]\",\"source\":\"mcp\"}"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, mcp.ts:298-299 reads `async ({ consumer, ...input }) => runSchema.parse(await backend.create({ ...input, requestedBy: consumer, source: \"mcp\" }))`. At base, MCP create_run never set requestedBy. The factory branches on that value: context.ts:276 `this.run.requestedBy === \"dependabot[bot]\" && ...dependabotRouting === \"free_first\"` and engine.ts:1407-1408 `dependencyUpdate: ... || ctx.run.requestedBy === \"dependabot[bot]\"`. The impact is limited. Delivery still needs a verified GitHub webhook with source github (delivery.ts:44-58). The review prompt only gains a dependency-check paragraph; the other review criteria stay. POST /api/runs (http.ts:409-411) already accepts any requestedBy from the same loopback callers that can reach /mcp. So this mixes a client label into an identity field rather than breaking a boundary."
  }
